  #line-at(283.61pt, 285.05pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 311.81pt, "A quantum-enhanced approach for fast temperature diagnostics in ultrashort laser surface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 326.33pt, "processing is introduced. The goal is to overcome the current limitations of the measuring ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 340.85pt, "methods, like influence of plasma emission, changes of emissivity during ablation, or time ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 355.37pt, "consuming pump-probe setup. The core principle of the described method is exploiting ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 369.89pt, "polarization anisotropy in entangled photon pairs. In this method, the idler photon interacts with ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 384.53pt, "the laser-affected material surface, while its entangled counterpart, the signal photon, is detected ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 399.05pt, "in a remote, undisturbed optical arm. The strong correlation of the photons’ polarizations after the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 413.57pt, "interaction that provides the idler photon measurement is crucial for the method. Temperature-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 428.11pt, "dependent changes in the complex refractive index modulate the reflectance of ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(491.86pt, 428.11pt, "p")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(497.86pt, 428.11pt, "- and ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(532.18pt, 428.11pt, "s")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(537.58pt, 428.11pt, "-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 442.75pt, "polarizations on the idler path, which in turn alter the coincidence-resolved polarization statistics ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 457.27pt, "of the signal photons. Using a Qiskit-based quantum model incorporating experimentally ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 471.79pt, "measured pump-probe reflectometry data, we show that this entanglement-assisted scheme ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 486.31pt, "enables remote reconstruction of rapid thermal dynamics during femtosecond laser ablation. This ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 500.83pt, "approach is not experimental, however experimental data embedded in the simulation algorithm ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 515.47pt, "were taken from literature sources to model a realistic material response. Due to the binary and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 529.99pt, "highly discrete nature of single-photon events, classical sliding-window analysis exhibits strong ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 544.51pt, "shot noise or temporal inertia. To overcome this limitation, we apply a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 559.03pt, "regression network that extracts implicit anisotropy information from the photon bitstream. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 573.55pt, "neural-network approach yields an order-of-magnitude reduction in temperature noise, enhances ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 588.19pt, "signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), and improves temporal accuracy to the nanosecond scale. These ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 602.74pt, "results demonstrate that entangled-photon polarization anisotropy, combined with machine-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 617.26pt, "learning analysis, offers a promising route toward undisturbed, background-rejected, high-speed ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 631.78pt, "temperature diagnostics in laser–matter interaction studies. ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(535.06pt, 740.976pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#666666"))
  #line-at(73.104pt, 81.98pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.384pt, 108.98pt, "Laser surface texturing (LST) using ultrashort pulses initiates a high temperature process, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 123.5pt, "such as spallation or phase explosion. Part of laser energy is absorbed under the surface in the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 138.02pt, "latent form of a liquid phase or later chemical changes, like oxidation of upper layers [1]–[3]. During ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 152.54pt, "high frequency laser pulsing, such accumulation processes become significant and are able to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 167.18pt, "change the quality and efficiency of LST. To evaluate the thermal changes between laser pulses ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 181.7pt, "it is possible to perform thermal measurements with fast IR-sensors in wave range 1-10 µm and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 196.22pt, "it gives the possibility to detect phase changes or even re-structuration of surface in real time [4]–")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 210.74pt, "[6]. The problem is in the correct evaluation of absolute value of temperature, because the emitted ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 225.26pt, "IR-thermal radiation intensity is affected by optical properties of the laser processed material, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 239.9pt, "plasma glow and non-homogeneous temperature distribution. On the other hand, laser surface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 254.45pt, "processing significantly modifies the optical properties of the material. This leads to a coupled ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 268.97pt, "problem in which temperature measurements depend on the optical properties, while the optical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 283.49pt, "properties themselves are affected by temperature changes through the emissivity [3], [7]. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 298.13pt, "existing methods, which are in use to bypass this principal limitation, are following the dynamics ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 312.65pt, "of IR-thermal radiation changes and using some plateaus as benchmarks which correspond to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 327.17pt, "fixed temperature points, like melting or solidification transitions. Another method is two-color ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 341.69pt, "measurement in which the relative changes of the IR-thermal signal on two (or more) sensors with ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 356.21pt, "different optical high pass and low pass filters are in use for temperature changes evaluation [6], ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 370.85pt, "[8], [9]. Such a method is based on the spectral distribution of IR described by Planck’s law, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 385.37pt, "followed by Wien’s displacement law [10]. A higher temperature increases emission at shorter ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 399.89pt, "wavelengths and alters the intensity ratio between the shorter and longer wavelength bands ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 414.41pt, "defined by the IR detector filters [11], [12]. But even this two-color pyrometry method is affected ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 428.95pt, "by optical changes to the irradiated surface, like phase, chemical or structural changes [6], [11], ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 443.59pt, "[12]. An alternative is in a fast measurement of optical changes by using an additional beam of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 458.11pt, "light with reflection from laser ablated spot and evaluation of optical properties changes as ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 472.63pt, "indicator of temperature changes [3], [13]. The method of the reflected beam intensity detection ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 487.15pt, "cannot be used for the direct temperature evaluation, because reflection changes in the laser spot ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 501.79pt, "interaction area not only by the temperature itself, but also by the surface deformation and its ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 516.31pt, "structure changes during ablation. An alternative approach is the application of fast polarization-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 530.83pt, "resolved thermometry using a pulsed pump–probe ellipsometry method. The temperature ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 545.35pt, "changes are then evaluated not from the intensity of the reflected beam, but from the analysis of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 559.87pt, "the changes in the p- and s-polarization components of the reflected beam. Ultrafast pump–probe ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 574.51pt, "ellipsometry is capable of evaluating temporal changes in optical properties and determining ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 589.03pt, "temperature variations during the laser ablation process, with a time resolution equal to the pulse ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 603.58pt, "duration—that is, on the picosecond or even femtosecond timescale [14], [15]. The limitations of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 618.1pt, "this method include the need for consequent sample movement to a fresh surface position for ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 632.74pt, "each time-step measurement. Additional challenges arise from changes in the reflection angle ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 647.26pt, "due to thermal modification of the sample surface, as well as from the separation of the reflected ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 661.78pt, "beam from ablation plasma emission, thermal radiation, and scattered light. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 688.3pt, "The improvement of the existing optical methods of the fast temperature measurement can ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 702.816pt, "be achieved by application of quantum effects, like entangled states with polarization and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 717.456pt, "coincidence detection. Such a method of ‘entangled twin-photon’ ellipsometer [16]–[19] has the ")
]

