#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 10.5pt), 259pt, 530.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(535.06pt, 740.976pt, "3 ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81pt, "separate arms of the ellipsometer and the light beams traverse them independently in different ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 95.66pt, "directions. This allows any desired polarization manipulation in the untouched signal arm of the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 110.18pt, "entangled twin-photon ellipsometer, independently from changing of incidence angle on the idler ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 124.7pt, "arm [16], [19]. In this paper, we discuss a possible application of the same twin-photon ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 139.22pt, "ellipsometer in the context of time-resolved temperature measurement. Unlike the mentioned twin-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 153.86pt, "photon ellipsometer the principle, which is described in this paper, involved a fixed polarization ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 168.38pt, "basis in combination of quantum entangled photons and polarization-resolved thermometry.  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(73.104pt, 221.3pt, "2. ")
  #line-at(95.544pt, 221.3pt, "Coincidence and temperature measurements with two-photon entangled state  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 247.82pt, "2.1. Detection of temperature changes by polarization anisotropy  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.82pt, 274.61pt, "In this article, the temperature measurement process is investigated through a proposed ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 289.13pt, "simulation. Although it is not a direct experiment, the simulation algorithm incorporates ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 303.65pt, "experimental data from literature sources to model a realistic material response. A nonlinear ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 318.17pt, "crystal can generate pairs of entangled photons through the process known as spontaneous ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 332.69pt, "parametric down-conversion (SPDC) [16], [20]. In this process, an incoming high-energy pump ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 347.33pt, "photon interacts with the nonlinear medium and, with a small probability, “splits” into two lower-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 361.85pt, "energy photons commonly called the ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(249.65pt, 361.85pt, "signal")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(278.33pt, 361.85pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(301.49pt, 361.85pt, "idler")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(322.25pt, 361.85pt, ". Energy and momentum conservation ensure ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 376.37pt, "strict correlations between these two photons, while the nonlinear interaction preserves the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 390.89pt, "quantum coherence necessary for entanglement. Under appropriate phase-matching conditions, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 405.53pt, "determined by the crystal orientation, refractive indices, and pump wavelength, the emitted photon ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 420.05pt, "pair emerges in a well-defined pure quantum state. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 446.59pt, "When the crossed crystals are operated in the type-I SPDC configuration, both the signal ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 461.11pt, "and idler photons are produced with the same polarization. This leads naturally to a Bell-like ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 475.63pt, "entangled state in which the joint polarization of the pair is perfectly correlated, even though the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 490.27pt, "polarization of each photon individually remains undefined until a measurement is performed. A ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 504.79pt, "typical state produced in this geometry can be written, for example, as: ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(225.89pt, 534.07pt, "∣𝛹⟩=")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(261.65pt, 527.59pt, "1")
  #line-at(259.13pt, 540.19pt, "√2")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
