// arXiv source page 11
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-11.png", width: 459.5pt), 76pt, 74pt))[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(528.94pt, 740.976pt, "11 ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 251.9pt, "Fig. 7. Temperature evaluation by calibration of polarization anisotropy and averaging window ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 266.45pt, "duration equal to 40 ns. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 292.97pt, "The achieved results have a representative tendency of temperature changes, but the root ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 307.49pt, "mean square amplitude of noise reaches up to 200 °C. In that situation the signal to noise ratio is ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 322.13pt, "low, down to 10 dB and it is difficult to extract the clear dynamics of temperature changes. If the ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 336.89pt, "𝐻")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(79.104pt, 336.89pt, "-photons absorption increases, the situation becomes worse - the measured data will be ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 351.41pt, "represented as a hundred of thousands long vector of zeros with randomly distributed “1”. In this ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 366.05pt, "situation higher physical affection of material brings higher noise and extraction of positive signals ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 380.57pt, "becomes more difficult. For the correct evaluation of temperature changes from the binary ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 395.09pt, "measurement data, it is preferable to use an alternative data-processing method. In the next ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 409.61pt, "section, we describe an algorithm specifically designed to extract hidden information from long ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 424.13pt, "one-dimensional data vectors. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 477.19pt, "3. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 477.19pt, "Neuronetwork processing of optical anisotropy binary vector ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.82pt, 503.83pt, "A fundamental dilemma arises from the discrete character of the measurement data in ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 518.35pt, "entangled photon polarization anisotropy, which are represented as H- and V-states in the form of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 532.99pt, "a long binary vector [01101…00101] (Fig. 1). When the optical anisotropy is evaluated as the local ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 547.51pt, "density of a binary vector, the size of the evaluation window becomes critical: a longer window ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 562.03pt, "decreases the measurement resolution, whereas a shorter window increases noise due to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 576.55pt, "fluctuations in the density arising from the binary switching between ‘1’ and ‘0’ (Fig. 7.). ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 603.1pt, "In the case of one-dimensional binary vector analysis, the application of a neural network ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 617.74pt, "is more suitable, particularly when the information about optical anisotropy is encoded indirectly ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 632.26pt, "and cannot be easily extracted using analytical methods. It should be emphasized that the primary ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 646.78pt, "objective of this study is not the development or deep optimization of machine-learning (ML) ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 661.3pt, "models, but rather the evaluation of whether a standard ML approach can provide a more robust ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 675.94pt, "alternative to the classical sliding-window method for temperature reconstruction under realistic ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 690.46pt, "noise conditions. In this context, the ML model is treated as a representative data-driven estimator ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 704.976pt, "and is directly compared with the analytical sliding-window approach using identical input data. ")
]

