  #line-at(70.824pt, 81pt, "The analytical temperature evaluation employed the same statistical estimation formula, but ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 95.66pt, "utilized a 40 ns window size, which is two times wider in comparison to the 18 ns window in the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 110.18pt, "MLP model. Despite this adjustment, the analytical method yielded a high root mean square ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 124.7pt, "amplitude, about 20% of the positive signal amplitude. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 151.22pt, "In contrast, in the MLP-based method of temperature evaluation the noise suppression is ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 165.86pt, "achieved by extraction of hidden information. It can be explained in the way that each combination ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 180.38pt, "of “1” and “0” corresponds to its original temperature evaluation, with high sensitivity to local ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 194.9pt, "density in a bin. As a result, the MLP achieves an order-of-magnitude lower RMS noise ~20 °C ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 209.42pt, "and a much higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) near 26 dB (Fig. 9). ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 521.23pt, "Fig. 9. MLP-reconstructed temperature (orange line) compared with the reference temperature ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 535.75pt, "(solid blue line). ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 588.79pt, "In the model presented here, the input is a vector consisting of six hundred statistical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 603.46pt, "estimation bins (Eq. 8). It decreases the neural network size for applications on regular CPU cores. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 617.98pt, "The full network architecture can be described mathematically as consequence function: ")
