  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(102.02pt, 169.1pt, " is the point reconstructed temperature curve. The MLP model used here contains ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 183.74pt, "approximately ~7.8 million trainable parameters. It is a middle-sized model with the capacity of the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 198.26pt, "non-convolutional architecture suited to converting coarse-grained photon statistics into ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 212.78pt, "temperature evaluation with 1 ns detailing (Fig. 9, orange line). ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 239.42pt, "To evaluate the performance of the proposed neural-network-based temperature reconstruction, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 253.94pt, "it was directly compared with a classical sliding-window method applied to the same binary ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 268.49pt, "coincidence data. The sliding-window approach estimates temperature locally by averaging ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 283.01pt, "coincidence statistics within a finite temporal window, whereas the ML model performs a global ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 297.53pt, "mapping from the full input vector to the temperature evolution, implicitly accounting for ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 312.17pt, "correlations and noise patterns in the data. The reconstruction accuracy of both methods was ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 326.69pt, "quantified using the root-mean-square error (RMSE) [32], defined as:  ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
