#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(528.94pt, 740.976pt, "17 ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81pt, "with measurements of the reflected-light intensity and fast pyrometry along a single optical path. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 95.66pt, "The binary character of the measurement provides additional benefits beyond the evaluation of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 110.18pt, "coincidence events alone, enabling the analysis of background-related detection statistics as well. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 124.7pt, "In particular, non-coincidence events, in which an idler-side detection occurs without a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 139.22pt, "corresponding entangled partner in the signal arm, can indicate additional thermal emission from ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 153.86pt, "the laser-heated surface. Conversely, coincidence-lost events (where a signal photon is detected ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 168.38pt, "without a registered idler photon) may arise from increased absorption, scattering, or surface ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 182.9pt, "deformation on the idler side. The temporal evolution of the rates of such ‘lost’ events can therefore ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 197.42pt, "serve as an additional indicator of laser-induced modifications of the processed surface. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 211.94pt, "Incorporating these auxiliary detection statistics enhances the overall dataset and improves the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 226.58pt, "precision of optical temperature measurements. Such a hybrid approach can further extend the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 241.1pt, "understanding of fast physical processes initiated by the interaction of ultrashort laser pulses with ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 255.65pt, "matter. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.824pt, 308.57pt, "5. Conclusion ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.824pt, 335.33pt, "In this article, polarization anisotropy of entangled photons is introduced as a promising ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 349.85pt, "approach for fast temperature measurement with remote detection. The main advantages include ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 364.37pt, "suppression of disturbing effects such as changes in emissivity of the laser-processed material ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 378.89pt, "and plasma emission, as well as potential real-time response in the nanosecond time range. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 393.53pt, "method was evaluated on the modeling of ultrashort laser pulse interaction with stainless steel. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 408.05pt, "The principle of temperature measurement based on remote polarization detection, triggered by ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 422.57pt, "reflected idler photon events, was demonstrated. Such separation of the triggering and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 437.11pt, "polarization measurement stages reduces the requirements for local measurement techniques, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 451.63pt, "as only a single-photon bucket detector is needed in the laser interaction zone for idler photon ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 466.27pt, "registration. A key advantage of the proposed method is that temperature is derived from the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 480.79pt, "coincidence rate rather than from the absolute signal intensity. This enables suppression of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 495.31pt, "parasitic effects associated with idler reflection, such as surface deformation, scattering from ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 509.83pt, "roughness, or emissivity variations of the laser-irradiated material. ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 536.47pt, "A quantum circuit model was employed to simulate the measurement principle. First, a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 550.99pt, "Hadamard gate was applied to the signal qubit to prepare a superposition state, followed by a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 565.51pt, "CNOT gate to generate entanglement. Coincidence detection was then realized by measuring ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 580.03pt, "both qubits. In the used scheme, optical anisotropy is detected not by the intensity of the reflected ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 594.55pt, "signal, but by the relative amount of H- and V-state remotely detected signal photons. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 609.22pt, "simulation indicates that analytical recognition of temperature by the sliding-window method has ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 623.74pt, "inherent limitations due to the binary nature of single-photon measurements. The selected short ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 638.26pt, "window size (40 ns), required for good temporal resolution, leads to a relatively low signal-to-noise ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 652.78pt, "ratio (~10 dB). ")
  #line-at(106.82pt, 679.42pt, "An alternative method of results processing was investigated, based on a regression ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 693.936pt, "neural network applied to entangled photon sequence analysis. This approach improves the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 708.456pt, "effective SNR (26 dB versus 10 dB) and provides more stable reconstruction under noisy ")
]

